From: allolive <160342668+allolive@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] drm/panel: icna3520: add 30..120Hz modes

The ICNA3520 is a command-mode panel, so DRM mode timings do not drive scanout
directly. The refresh rate is set by the front porch handed to the DDIC in the
sub-120Hz init path: bytes 12..13 of 0xB3 are 0x07D6 == 2006, exactly the 60Hz
mode's front porch. Every rate is the same pixel clock with a different porch.

Measured on an Odin 3 via the DPU vsync counter and dsi_isr, 50/75/100Hz land
exactly, so the DDIC derives its refresh linearly from that field:

    vfp = round(267911000 / (1129 * rate)) - 1949

which reproduces the stock entries exactly (120Hz -> 28, 60Hz -> 2006).

A rate only works if it has an entry here: icna35xx_get_current_mode()
drm_mode_match()es the active mode against this table, and a mode not in it
falls through to index 0 (120Hz), leaving the DDIC untouched.

== Residue constraint ==

A front porch congruent to 2 mod 4 (vtotal congruent to 3 mod 4) makes the
panel render visibly elevated black levels. Found by bisecting a user-visible
artifact: confirmed bad at 31, 33, 34, 43, 46, 49, 52, 55, 57, 58, 62, 63, 72,
73, 74, 83 and 86Hz, and clean at every rate tested outside the class. A
contiguous sweep of 30..46 matched the predicted set exactly, and 83/86 were
predicted before being tested.

60Hz is the sole exception: it sits in the class and looks fine. That fits the
else branch writing the vendor's 60Hz calibration -- the other 0xB3 fields and
0x48 are tuned for 60, so at exactly 60 nothing is mismatched. It also means
the stock 60Hz porch of 2006, itself in the class, was never a defect.

vtotal is nudged one line to leave the class, costing at most 1.6ppm of rate
accuracy -- about one frame of drift every five hours. Every rate keeps its
integer drm_mode_vrefresh() slot. The mechanism is undocumented to us; that it
is one residue class in four rather than a parity suggests a hardware alignment
requirement rather than anything about frequency.

== NTSC slots ==

    content    slot   panel rate   ratio
    23.976fps    72   71.9280Hz     3:1
    24.000fps    48   48.0000Hz     2:1
    29.97fps     60   59.9400Hz     2:1
    30.000fps    30   30.0000Hz     1:1

drm_mode_vrefresh() is integer, so each slot holds one mode. Putting 23.976 in
slot 24 would have cost exact 24; at 3:1 it lands in slot 72 with the same
precision, leaving 48 exact. .clock is given per mode because the rates are no
longer all integers, so the table macro takes a clock rather than a rate.

Only modes[0] (120Hz) is DRM_MODE_TYPE_PREFERRED, so everything else is opt-in.

120Hz is left exactly as the panel shipped it: same porch (28), same vtotal
(1977), and the clock pinned to 267843 so it matches the original expression
verbatim. It also takes the cur_vrefresh == 120 branch, whose 0xB3 payload is
fixed rather than porch-derived, so the DDIC is configured identically to stock.

Note for userspace: gamescope's refresh doubling picks the highest advertised
rate that is an integer multiple of the target FPS, so advertising everything
makes 50fps select 100Hz and 30fps select 120Hz. Keep the advertised list
curated or apply the gamescope patch preferring an exact rate.

The 0xB3 write moves to mipi_dsi_generic_write_multi() because the _seq_ macro
stashes its varargs in a `static const u8[]` and cannot carry a runtime value.

Unverified: 0x48 is 0x00 at 120Hz and 0x10 below it, assumed a generic
low-refresh select rather than 60Hz-specific, and reused as-is.

--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/panel/panel-chipone-icna35xx.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/panel/panel-chipone-icna35xx.c
@@ -200,7 +200,8 @@
 	u8 bl_payload[] = { 0x51, brightness >> 8, brightness & 0xff };
 
 	int cur_mode = icna35xx_get_current_mode(pinfo);
-	int cur_vrefresh = drm_mode_vrefresh(&pinfo->desc->modes[cur_mode]);
+	const struct drm_display_mode *cur = &pinfo->desc->modes[cur_mode];
+	int cur_vrefresh = drm_mode_vrefresh(cur);
 
 	pinfo->dsi->mode_flags |= MIPI_DSI_MODE_LPM;
 
@@ -221,11 +222,22 @@
                 0x00, 0xD8, 0x00, 0x1C, 0x00, 0x4C);
 	}
 	else {
+		/*
+		 * Bytes 12..13 of 0xB3 are the front porch the DDIC derives its
+		 * refresh from. Measured on an Odin 3: 50, 75 and 100Hz all land
+		 * exactly, so the relationship is linear across the range.
+		 */
+		u16 vfp = cur->vsync_start - cur->vdisplay;
+		u8 b3_payload[] = {
+			0xB3,
+			0x00, 0xDB, 0x00, 0x1C, 0x00, 0x1C, 0x00, 0x00,
+			0xDB, 0x00, 0x1C, (u8)(vfp >> 8), (u8)(vfp & 0xff), 0x00
+		};
+
 		mipi_dsi_generic_write_seq_multi(&dsi_ctx, 0x48, 0x10);
 		mipi_dsi_generic_write_seq_multi(&dsi_ctx, 0x9F, 0x00);
-		mipi_dsi_generic_write_seq_multi(&dsi_ctx, 0xB3, 
-                0x00, 0xDB, 0x00, 0x1C, 0x00, 0x1C, 0x00, 0x00,
-				0xDB, 0x00, 0x1C, 0x07, 0xD6, 0x00);
+		mipi_dsi_generic_write_multi(&dsi_ctx, b3_payload,
+					     sizeof(b3_payload));
 	}
 
 	mipi_dsi_generic_write_seq_multi(&dsi_ctx, 0x9F, 0x01);
@@ -387,31 +399,127 @@
 	}
 };
 
+/*
+ * Every rate is the same pixel clock with a stretched vertical front porch;
+ * the DDIC derives its refresh from that porch (see icna3520_init_sequence):
+ *     vfp = round(267911000 / (1129 * rate)) - 1949
+ *
+ * Constraint found on hardware: a front porch congruent to 2 mod 4 (vtotal
+ * congruent to 3 mod 4) makes the panel render elevated black levels.
+ * Confirmed bad at 31, 33, 34, 43, 46, 49, 52, 55, 57, 58, 62, 63, 72, 73,
+ * 74, 83 and 86Hz; every rate tested outside the class is clean. 60Hz is the
+ * sole exception -- it sits in the class and looks fine, which fits the else
+ * branch of the init sequence writing the vendor's 60Hz calibration. vtotal
+ * is nudged one line to avoid the class, costing at most 1.6ppm.
+ *
+ * Two slots carry an NTSC rate rather than the exact integer so film and
+ * broadcast content runs judder-free; userspace still sees 72 and 60 via
+ * drm_mode_vrefresh(). .clock is per mode as the rates are not all integers.
+ */
+#define ICNA3520_MODE(clk, vfp) {					\
+	.clock = (clk),							\
+	.hdisplay = 1080,						\
+	.hsync_start = 1080 + 24,					\
+	.hsync_end = 1080 + 24 + 1,					\
+	.htotal = 1080 + 24 + 1 + 24,					\
+	.vdisplay = 1920,						\
+	.vsync_start = 1920 + (vfp),					\
+	.vsync_end = 1920 + (vfp) + 1,					\
+	.vtotal = 1920 + (vfp) + 1 + 28,				\
+}
